The '''2009 FA Cup final''' was the 128th [[FA Cup Final|final]] of the world's oldest domestic [[association football|football]] cup competition, the [[FA Cup]]. The final was played at [[Wembley Stadium]] in London on 30 May 2009 and marked the third time that the final has been staged at the stadium since it was rebuilt. The match was contested by [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], who beat [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] 2–1 in their semi-final, and [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] who beat [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] 4–2 on penalties after a 0–0 draw after extra time. After [[Louis Saha]] opened the scoring after just 25 seconds, which is the second fastest ever goal in an FA Cup Final, [[Didier Drogba]] equalised in the 21st minute before [[Frank Lampard]] scored the winner with 19 minutes left to play to give Chelsea their fifth FA Cup success.<ref name="bbc_sahagoal">{{cite news |first=Caroline |last=Cheese |title=Live text – FA Cup final |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/8075105.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=30 May 2009 |access-date=30 May 2009 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20090531012146/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/8075105.stm| archive-date= 31 May 2009 | url-status= live}}</ref>

==Background==
Chelsea went into the match as four-time FA Cup winners, having previously won in [[1970 FA Cup final|1970]], [[1997 FA Cup final|1997]], [[2000 FA Cup final|2000]] and [[2007 FA Cup final|2007]], while Everton had won the competition five times previously, having won in [[1906 FA Cup final|1906]], [[1933 FA Cup final|1933]], [[1966 FA Cup final|1966]], [[1984 FA Cup final|1984]] and [[1995 FA Cup final|1995]]. Coincidentally, both teams' most recent titles came in the FA Cup, both teams beating [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in the final; Everton in 1995 and Chelsea in 2007.

The two teams drew 0–0 in both meetings during the [[2008–09 Premier League]] season, at [[Goodison Park]] in December and at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]] in April.<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/7775702.stm Everton 0–0 Chelsea]</ref><ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/8000558.stm Chelsea 0–0 Everton]</ref> Prior to the 2009 final, the two teams had never met in the [[FA Cup Final]]; their most recent meeting in the FA Cup came in the Fourth Round of the [[FA Cup 2005–06|2005–06 competition]], when Chelsea won 4–1 in a replay at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]] after a 1–1 draw at [[Goodison Park]]. In 157 matches between the two sides in all competitions, Chelsea had recorded 61 wins, Everton had won 49, and 47 matches finished as draws.

