==Pre-match==
===Ticketing===
Everton and Chelsea were each allocated 25,109 tickets for the final. Having sold over 32,000 for their semi-final win over Manchester United, Everton were unhappy and disappointed with this allocation. The match was Everton's first FA Cup final in 14 years and Everton chief executive Robert Elstone predicted that the club could have sold in excess of 70,000 tickets for this game. Chelsea also said that the size of their allocation meant that it was unlikely that there would be sufficient tickets for all of their hospitality and non-hospitality season ticket holders.<ref>{{cite news |title=Everton issue Cup tickets warning |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/8015583.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=28 April 2009 |access-date=7 May 2009 | archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20090427183829/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/8015583.stm| archive-date= 27 April 2009 | url-status= live}}</ref>

Club Wembley ten-year season ticket holders were also guaranteed a ticket for the match. After those tickets were allocated, 70% of the remaining tickets were distributed amongst the clubs that had competed in the [[FA Cup 2008–09|2008–09 competition]], with the other 30% given to those involved in grassroots football and the "football family".<ref>{{cite news|title=Cup Final ticket allocations |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/FACFinal2009_Ticketdetails.aspx |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090528042733/http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/FACFinal2009_Ticketdetails.aspx |url-status=dead |archive-date=28 May 2009 |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=23 April 2009 |access-date=30 May 2009 }}</ref>

===Kits===
Since both finalists' first-choice kits are predominantly blue, a coin was [[coin flipping|tossed]] to determine which team would have the first choice of kit. Everton won the toss and chose to wear their traditional blue kit, meaning that Chelsea would wear their all-yellow third-choice kit.<ref>{{cite news|title=Yellow is the colour |url=http://www.chelseafc.com/page/LatestNews/0,,10268~1637219,00.html |work=ChelseaFC.com |publisher=Chelsea F.C. |date=23 April 2009 |access-date=23 April 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090426035002/http://www.chelseafc.com/page/LatestNews/0%2C%2C10268~1637219%2C00.html |archive-date=26 April 2009 |url-status=live }}</ref> Chelsea were nevertheless given the "home" [[Changeroom|dressing room]], i.e. that used by the [[England national football team|England national team]].<ref>{{cite episode |title=Chelsea 2{{ndash}}1 Everton |series=FA Cup Live |url=http://www.itv.com/sport/football/news/facup/matchreports/report-chelsea-v-everton-456172118.html |network=ITV |station=[[ITV1]] |location=London |airdate=30 May 2009 |minutes=86:00 |access-date=14 June 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090603230200/http://www.itv.com/sport/football/news/facup/matchreports/report-chelsea-v-everton-456172118.html |archive-date=3 June 2009 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

===Match ball===
The official match ball of the 2009 FA Cup Final was the ''Umbro Dynamis''. The ''Dynamis'' uses a 20-panel configuration, as opposed to a more traditional 32-panel design, which allegedly makes the ball faster. The surface of the ball is made from a Teijin microfibre material. The ''Dynamis'' was also used in the 2008 final, but the 2009 version has a gold colourway.<ref>{{cite news|title=The number one ball for The Final |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/CupFinalMatchBall.aspx |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090612063831/http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/CupFinalMatchBall.aspx |url-status=dead |archive-date=12 June 2009 |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=29 May 2009 |access-date=30 May 2009 }}</ref>

===Opening ceremony===
The traditional FA Cup Final song, ''[[Abide With Me]]'', was performed by the [[London Community Gospel Choir]].<ref>{{cite news|title='Abide With Me' out now |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/AbideWithMe_outnow.aspx |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090614140336/http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/AbideWithMe_outnow.aspx |url-status=dead |archive-date=14 June 2009 |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=27 May 2009 |access-date=30 May 2009 }}</ref> The [[FA Cup#Trophies|trophy]] was then brought out onto the field, followed by the two teams, before the Chief Guest of the final – former [[Secretary General of the United Nations]] [[Kofi Annan]]<ref>{{cite news|title=Kofi Annan to attend FA Cup Final |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/FACFinal2009_KofiAnnan.aspx |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090615174458/http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/FACFinal2009_KofiAnnan.aspx |url-status=dead |archive-date=15 June 2009 |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=25 April 2009 |access-date=30 May 2009 }}</ref> – was introduced to the managers and players of both teams, as well as the match officials. The introductions were immediately followed by the singing of the national anthem, "[[God Save the Queen]]", sung by [[Britain's Got Talent (series 2)|Britain's Got Talent 2008]] finalist, 13-year-old [[Faryl Smith]], making her the youngest performer of the national anthem at the FA Cup final.<ref>{{cite news |first=Stuart |last=Mawhinney |title=Faryl's got the talent for The Final |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/FACupFinal_FarylSmith.aspx |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090614150814/http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/FACupFinal_FarylSmith.aspx |url-status=dead |archive-date=14 June 2009 |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=28 May 2009 |access-date=30 May 2009 }}</ref>

==Match==
===Team selection===
{{unreferenced section|date=June 2014}}
Everton were without long-term injury victims [[Phil Jagielka]], [[Mikel Arteta]], [[Yakubu Aiyegbeni]], [[Victor Anichebe]] and [[Nuno Valente]]. On-loan Brazilian striker [[Jô]] was cup-tied. [[Andy van der Meyde]], who set up the winning goal in the fourth round tie with Liverpool, had since been released by the club. This meant that there was a place on the Everton bench for 17-year-old winger [[Jose Baxter]].

===Summary===
====First half====
[[Louis Saha]] scored for Everton with a left-footed shot to the bottom-left corner just 25 seconds into the match.<ref name="full_time"/> The previous fastest goal was by [[Bob Chatt]] of [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] 30 seconds into the [[1895 FA Cup final|1895 final]].<ref name="bbc_sahagoal"/> It would the fastest goal scored in FA Cup final history until the [[2023 FA Cup final|2023 final]], when [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]]'s [[İlkay Gündoğan]] scored after 13 seconds.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/football/story/_/id/37787663/man-city-ilkay-gundogan-scores-fastest-ever-fa-cup-final-goal |access-date=2026-10-08 |website=www.espn.co.uk|title=Man City's Ilkay Gundogan scores fastest-ever FA Cup final goal|last=Associated Press}}</ref> [[Tony Hibbert]] received a yellow card for tripping [[Florent Malouda]] in the eighth minute.<ref name="full_time"/> [[Didier Drogba]] levelled the match for Chelsea in the 21st minute with a header set up by a left-wing cross from Malouda.<ref name="full_time"/>

====Second half====
Tony Hibbert was replaced by [[Lars Jacobsen]] for Everton to begin the second half,<ref name="full_time"/> and two minutes in, Toffees captain Phil Neville was booked.<ref name="full_time"/> Chelsea replaced [[Michael Essien]] with [[Michael Ballack]] in the 61st minute,<ref name="full_time"/> followed two minutes later by a yellow card for [[Mikel John Obi]].<ref name="full_time"/> Frank Lampard scored the winning goal for Chelsea in the 72nd minute with a left foot shot.<ref name="full_time"/> Five minutes later, a shot from Malouda appeared to crash down off the bar and over the line, but it was not given.<ref name="full_time"/> Everton replaced Saha with James Vaughan at that point.<ref name="full_time"/> Dan Gosling was the last substitute for Everton in the 81st minute, replacing Leon Osman.<ref name="full_time"/> Lampard received a yellow card in the 84th minute, while in the fourth minute of added time, Leighton Baines of Everton was booked.<ref name="full_time"/>

