====Records====
*[[Louis Saha]] scored the fastest ever FA Cup Final goal when he drove home with just 25 seconds on the clock to put Everton ahead.<ref>{{cite news |first=David |last=Barber |title=Early goals and Chatt rooms |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/EarlyGoalsChattRooms.aspx |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=1 June 2009 |access-date=2 June 2009 }}</ref> This was later broken in [[2023 FA Cup final|2023]] when [[İlkay Gündoğan]] scored after 12 seconds for Manchester City against Manchester United.<ref name=":1" />
*[[Ashley Cole]] collected his fifth FA Cup winners' medal, and became the first man to win the cup five times since the 19th century.<ref>{{cite news|first=James |last=Masters |title=History man Cole |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/AshleyCole_Final.aspx |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090612111615/http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2009/AshleyCole_Final.aspx |url-status=dead |archive-date=12 June 2009 |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=30 May 2009 |access-date=2 June 2009 }}</ref>
*Everton's defeat meant that they finished as FA Cup runners-up for the eighth time, more than any other club.<ref>{{cite web|title=Cup Final Statistics |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/History/CupFinalResults.aspx |archive-url=https://archive.today/20090616021730/http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/History/CupFinalResults.aspx |url-status=dead |archive-date=16 June 2009 |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |access-date=2 June 2009 }}</ref>

